{
  "summary": "The previous rounds have reached reasonable consensus on decomposition strategy but share a critical blind spot: they treat the refactor as primarily a code organization problem when the actual blocking issue is the absence of a testable boundary between pipeline orchestration logic and Textual's runtime. Rounds 4 and 5 correctly identify that @work(thread=True) methods cannot simply be extracted to free functions, but the proposed workarounds (call_from_thread bridges, message buses, mixin classes) all preserve the fundamental coupling — they just move it. The real critique is that none of the proposals grapple with the fact that the 57 call_from_thread invocations aren't incidental wiring; they ARE the application's concurrency model, and any refactoring that doesn't first establish a clean async boundary (e.g., a simple callback-based progress reporter injected into the worker methods, replacing the direct call_from_thread(self._log_line, ...) pattern) will just redistribute the coupling across more files without reducing it. Additionally, all five rounds underestimate a simpler, higher-leverage first step that none mention: the _run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, and _run_restart_async methods contain 200+ lines of nearly identical boilerplate (lock acquisition, AgentStatus setup, retry loops, error handling, rate limit recovery, finally-block cleanup). Before any module extraction, this duplication should be collapsed into a single _run_worker_async dispatcher parameterized by phase — this is a pure refactoring within PipelineApp that requires no new modules, no architectural decisions, and immediately eliminates ~150 lines while making the eventual extraction target smaller and more uniform. The discussion has been over-indexing on module boundaries and under-indexing on reducing the actual complexity of what would be extracted."
}
